  "query_preview": "model guidance continues to come into somewhat better \nalignment with the developing coastal low off the Carolinas by \nWednesday and Thursday in response to phasing jet energy aloft as \ndual upper lows diving out of the northern Plains and across the \nlower Mississippi Valley respectively eventually merge over the \neastern part of the country. Light rain will spread north into the \nregion Wednesday... being stunted initially by the strong high \npressure passing to the north of the region. As the high shifts east \ninto New England an inverted surface trough will lift into the Ohio \nValley with moisture advecting north around the front side of the \nsouthern upper low. Expect scattered light rain to develop as a \nresult on Wednesday and persist into Thanksgiving Day as forcing \nassociated with the northern stream upper low moves over the region.\n\nRemain a bit skeptical at potential precipitation amounts with the \ndeeper moisture plume and stronger forcing aloft being confined to \nareas closer to the developing coastal low over the Carolinas and \nMid Atlantic region. Damp and showery conditions will certainly \nlinger for Thanksgiving Day but overall precipitation coverage and \nintensity remain highly uncertain considering above thoughts. Precip \ntype should largely be in the form of rain although low level \nthermals do support the potential for a few snowflakes to mix in at \ntimes.\n\nThe low pressure will kick out into New England and the Canadian \nMaritimes into Friday with precipitation ending over the region \nThursday night. Broad upper level trough will however persist across \nthe Great Lakes and Ohio Valley into next weekend keeping \ntemperatures seasonably cool with clouds at times as weak \ndisturbances pass through the region.",
